        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Oliguria—production of an abnormally small amount of urine. Several proposed definitions; in hydrated patients with good perfusion, urine output (UOP) <1.0 mL/kg/h = absolute oliguria. Acute kidney injury (AKI) is typically polyuric, where UOP >2 mL/kg/h is expected.",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anuria—limited/no urine formation (UOP <0.08 mL/kg/h).",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Physiologic (prerenal) oliguria—kidneys limit water loss during state of reduced renal perfusion to preserve fluid and electrolyte balance. High plasma osmolality or low circulating fluid volume stimulate antidiuretic hormone (ADH) synthesis and release, which induces formation of small quantity of concentrated urine.",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pathologic (renal) oliguria—severe renal parenchymal impairment. Factors include: high resistance in afferent glomerular vessels, low glomerular permeability, back leak of filtrate from damaged renal tubules, renal intratubular obstruction, and extensive loss of nephrons resulting in marked reduction of glomerular filtrate produced.",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anuria—may be renal or postrenal origin. Severe renal disease occasionally causes anuria. Mechanisms are the same as for pathologic oliguria (e.g., urinary tract obstruction (UTO) or excretory pathway rupture).",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal—inability to adequately eliminate wastes and water; hyperkalemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urologic—obstruction-induced distension of the collecting system. Increased risk of urinary tract infection (UTI) due to failure to empty bladder.",
              "pdf_page": 1073,
              "printed_page": 991,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GENETICS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
