    "MEDICATIONS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "canonical_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diuretics—indicated after establishing euvolemia in patients with renal oliguria. Diuretic-induced UOP facilitates fluid and electrolyte therapy and may imply less severe kidney injury.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Administration of diuretics before restoring adequate renal perfusion is counterproductive and may promote renal injury.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Avoid diuretic-induced dehydration.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Furosemide 2–4 mg/kg IV—often used first in patients with renal oliguria. Urinary flow should increase within 1 hour; if not, repeat at the same or double dosage. If diuresis ensues, administer to sustain diuresis (0.25–1 mg/kg/h or 2–4 mg/kg IV q8h). Reduction in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) may be seen with therapy.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Mannitol 0.25–1 g/kg IV can be given as a 10% or 20% solution over 15–20 minutes. Urinary flow should increase within 1 hour. Do not repeat if diuresis does not ensue; may cause excessive volume expansion. If diuresis ensues, mannitol may be continued as continuous infusion (1–2 mg/kg/h) or intermittent IV doses (0.25–0.5 g/kg every 4–6 hours) to sustain diuresis. Avoid mannitol when overhydration, pulmonary edema, or congestive heart failure present.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fenoldopam 0.1–0.8μg/kg/min, a selective dopamine A1 receptor antagonist, promotes diuresis in healthy animals and increases GFR in dogs. Evidence of efficacy in animals with renal disease is lacking.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CONTRAINDICATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nephrotoxic drugs.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PRECAUTIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PRECAUTIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "PRECAUTIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Administer fluids carefully to patients that are persistently oligoanuric to avoid over hydration. Do not continue to administer fluids to oligoanuric patients after their fluid volume deficit has been restored in the absence of a plan to prevent development of",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Azotemia and Uremia. Furosemide may promote the nephrotoxicity",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Chronic Kidney Disease. associated with aminoglycoside antibiotics.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hyperkalemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1074,
              "printed_page": 992,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
